= Díaz calls an election

Susana Díaz breaks her coalition with Izquierda Unida and calls an Andalusian election for 22 March, a year early. She wants her own mandate before Podemos grows any further, and before the general election. Everyone in Ferraz understands the other reason: a big win would make her the strongest person in the PSOE.

- @campaign_with_diaz: Campaign at her side across Andalusia
- @distance_diaz: Let her run her own campaign

@campaign_with_diaz
subtitle: Costs 1 in party funds.
on-arrival: {! Q.funds -= 1; Q.diaz_relation += 6; Q.regional_leaders_dissent -= 5; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.regionalMod(Q, 'Andalucía', 'psoe', 1.03); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } !}

The two of you share stages from Almería to Huelva. If she wins, you can share the credit; she will not forget that you came.

@distance_diaz
on-arrival: diaz_relation -= 6; sanchismo += 2

She runs as the face of the Andalusian PSOE, not of yours. The result will be hers alone.

@rivera_national
title: Ciudadanos goes national
new-page: true
tags: sp_event
view-if: rivera_seen = 0 and year = 2015 and month >= 2
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: rivera_seen = 1

= Ciudadanos goes national

Albert Rivera, until now known for opposing Catalan independence, takes Ciudadanos across Spain. Young, telegenic and free of corruption cases, he offers "sensible change": regeneration without Podemos's revolution. Disillusioned PP voters, and some of yours, are listening.

- @spare_part: Call Ciudadanos the PP's spare part
- @court_centre: Compete for the centre: clean institutions and economic credibility
- @ignore_cs: Ignore them: the real fight is with Podemos and the PP

@spare_part
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_cs -= 10; Q.progressives_dissent -= 2; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'working', 1); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, 0.3, 0); } !}

The label sticks, among your own voters at least.

@court_centre
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_cs += 3; Q.modernisers_dissent -= 3; Q.progressives_dissent += 3; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'middle', 2.5); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, -0.8, 0); } !}

You hold on to more of the professional middle class than the polls expected.

@ignore_cs
on-arrival: institutionalists_dissent -= 1

Ciudadanos keeps rising, mostly at the PP's expense, and some at yours.

@mayors_of_change
title: The mayors of change
new-page: true
tags: sp_event
view-if: mayors_seen = 0 and ((year = 2015 and month >= 6) or year >= 2016)
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: mayors_seen = 1

= The mayors of change

The municipal elections redraw the map of Spanish cities. Citizen platforms backed by Podemos come close in Madrid, with the former judge Manuela Carmena, and win Barcelona, with the housing activist Ada Colau. In both, they can only govern with the PSOE's votes. Elsewhere, PSOE mayors need Podemos.

- @back_mayors: Back the platforms in Madrid and Barcelona, and ask for their votes elsewhere
- @own_candidates: Insist on PSOE mayors wherever possible
- @let_pp: Abstain and let the most-voted lists govern

@back_mayors
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_up += 10; Q.rel_cs -= 4; Q.progressives_dissent -= 6; Q.regional_leaders_dissent += 4; Q.mayors_of_change = 1; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'young', 1); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, 0.5, 0.3); } !}

Carmena and Colau take office with socialist votes, and the PSOE wins dozens of city halls and regional governments with Podemos's support in return.

@own_candidates
on-arrival: rel_up -= 6; institutionalists_dissent -= 3; progressives_dissent += 3

Tough negotiations, city by city. Some deals fail, and the PP keeps a few cities it had lost.

@let_pp
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_up -= 12; Q.rel_pp += 4; Q.member_morale -= 8; Q.progressives_dissent += 10; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.transfer(Q, 'psoe', 'up', {students: 2, workers: 1.5, unemployed: 1.5}); } !}

The PP keeps Madrid. Your members cannot believe it, and many of your voters decide Podemos is the only real alternative.

@syriza
title: Syriza gives way
new-page: true
tags: sp_event
view-if: syriza_seen = 0 and ((year = 2015 and month >= 7) or year >= 2016)
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: syriza_seen = 1

= Syriza gives way

A week after the Greeks voted "no" to the bailout terms in a referendum, Alexis Tsipras signs a third bailout harsher than the one they rejected. Podemos had made Syriza its model. Its promise that austerity could simply be refused looks much less convincing.

- @realism: Make the case for realistic change within Europe
- @solidarity: Express solidarity with Greece, and blame Merkel's austerity
- @say_nothing: Say nothing

@realism
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_up -= 4; Q.institutionalists_dissent -= 2; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.transfer(Q, 'up', 'psoe', {workers: 1, professionals: 1, pensioners: 0.5}); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, -0.8, 0); } !}

"Change is possible, but it has to be real." Some of the voters who left for Podemos start to come back.

@solidarity
on-arrival: {! Q.rel_up += 4; Q.progressives_dissent -= 3; Q.modernisers_dissent += 2; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, 0.8, 0); } !}

The left of the party approves. It does little for the PSOE's credibility as a government in waiting.

@say_nothing
on-arrival: institutionalists_dissent -= 1

Podemos's troubles are its own.

@mas_plebiscite
title: Mas calls a "plebiscitary" election
new-page: true
